   "i": 138,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Typed restrictionism has faced significant criticism. To begin with, Tarski-style typing is very drastic: cases of self-reference unproblematically occur in natural languages, so declaring Liar sentences ill-formed seems unmotivated. Though Tarski aimed mainly at formal languages, his solution looks implausible for ordinary uses of ‘true’. Secondly, typed restrictionist theories are very weak: not even a platitude such as ‘it is true that it is true that ⟦M⟧ ’ can hold in them. One way to amelio",
   "md_text": "Typed restrictionism has faced significant criticism. To begin with, Tarski-style typing is very drastic: cases of self-reference unproblematically occur in natural languages, so declaring Liar sentences ill-formed seems unmotivated. Though Tarski aimed mainly at formal languages, his solution looks implausible for ordinary uses of ‘true’. Secondly, typed restrictionist theories are very weak: not even a platitude such as ‘it is true that it is true that ⟦M⟧ ’ can hold in them. One way to amelio"
